text:
@IrishguyG80  Australia also has 3phase which supports upto 22kW, the limit isn't the power supply it is the car, the car is limiting it

you bought a BYD Seal didn't you? Show it getting 22kW on AC, I bet it won't as the on-board charger only support 11kW 3phase or 7kW single phase, to get 22kW on single phase you would be pushing around 100amps

Even your BYD Seal will max out at 7kW on single phase, if 4kW is so slow why did you go the Seal and not the m3RWD which supports upto 170kW DC?

@almac9203 refers to a youtube video where the rear wheels of the AWD Seal were put on rollers (so no traction) to see if the front wheels would pull the car forward from a stand still.  It did not.
However BYD seemed to take the issue seriously and quickly reprogrammed the firmware to address it.  Its possible the car doesnt engage the front motor by design unless you're giving it the berries (my guess) or over a certain speed.
BYD so appear to be listening to feedback for the car and addressing problems and aesthetics concerns.

@MHWib-v7x I cancelled my Seal order due to the low ground clearance of this vehicle.
I've ordered a Tesla Model 3 LR AWD which sits a few centimeters higher in my steep driveway.
Make sure you are happy with the low(er) ground clearance in certain driveways, speed humps and those annoying (and variable height) wheel stops in parking spaces. The front bumper is pretty much guaranteed to scrape is certain situations. Not acceptable for my use.
